@node API
@chapter API

Here lies the core procedures for building and manipulating Goblins
programs!

Unless where stated, most code from this section can be found by
importing:

@example
(use-modules (goblins))
@end example

@menu
* Objects::
* Vats::
* Actormaps::
* OCapN::
* Persistence::
@end menu

@node Objects
@section Objects

Objects are the core of Goblins.
In general, you'll need to be within an ambient ``Goblins Context'' (TM)
in order to start programming against them, see @ref{Vats} for most event
loop type programming, or @ref{Actormaps} if you want to play with more low-level
control over transactional operations.
(However, most users will just use vats.)

@menu
* Spawning objects::
* Synchronous invocation::
* Asynchronous invocation::
@end menu

@node Spawning objects
@subsection Spawning objects

Objects can be spawned with the @samp{spawn} operator:

@example
(spawn CONSTRUCTOR ARGS ...)
;=> <local-refr>
@end example

where @samp{CONSTRUCTOR} is a procedure which itself returns a procedure
representing an object's current behavior.
The @samp{CONSTRUCTOR} is implicitly passed a first argument, traditionally
labeled @samp{bcom}, which can be used to change an object's behavior.
The @samp{ARGS} are passed as the remaining arguments to the constructor.

So for example, let's say we have the following constructor:

@example
(define* (^cell bcom #:optional val)  ; constructor (outer procedure)
  (case-lambda                        ; behavior    (inner procedure)
    (()                               ; 0-argument invocation (getter)
     val)                             ;   (return current value)
    ((new-val)                        ; 1-argument invocation (setter)
     (bcom (^cell bcom new-val)))))   ;   ("become" ^cell with new value)
@end example

And so@dots{}

@example
(define some-cell (spawn ^cell 42))
@end example

will bind @samp{val} to @samp{42}.

Let's look at the @samp{bcom} argument in depth.
This is a capability which allows the actor to change its own behavior.
It must be called in a tail position, since @samp{bcom} actually returns
a datastructure demonstrating that this object is choosing to change
its value (part of Goblins' quasi-functional design.)

@samp{bcom} also can take a second argument, which will be returned from
the object's invocation as a return value, allowing for both an object
change its own behavior and return a value at the same time during an
invocation.

The following actor represents a consumable object that returns a
string explaining its current status:

@example
(define (^drink bcom drink-name portions)
  (define (drinkable-beh portions)   ; "-beh" is a common suffix for "-behavior"
    (lambda ()
      (define portions-left
        (- portions 1))
      (if (zero? portions-left)
          (bcom empty-beh
                (format #f "*GLUG!* You drink your ~a. It's empty!"
                        drink-name))
          (bcom (drinkable-beh portions-left)
                (format #f "*GLUG!* You drink your ~a. Still some left!"
                        drink-name)))))
  (define (empty-beh)
    "Sadly, your glass appears to be empty!")
  (drinkable-beh portions))
@end example

Now, playing around at the REPL, we can see how this works:

@example
> ,vr (define root-beer (spawn ^drink "root beer" 3))
> ,vr ($ root-beer)
=> "*GLUG!* You drink your root beer. Still some left!"
> ,vr ($ root-beer)
=> "*GLUG!* You drink your root beer. Still some left!"
> ,vr ($ root-beer)
=> "*GLUG!* You drink your root beer. It's empty!"
> ,vr ($ root-beer)
=> "Sadly, your glass appears to be empty!"
@end example

As you can see above, @samp{bcom} is also a handy way to construct state
machines, as @samp{^drink} moves from drinkable to empty as it is consumed.

@node Synchronous invocation
@subsection Synchronous invocation

To synchronously invoke two objects within the same vat / actormap,
use @samp{$}:

@example
($ near-object args ...)
;=> something
@end example

Provide a synchronous call against the current message handler of
@samp{NEAR-REFR}, which must be a @emph{near} @samp{live-refr?}
in the same vat as the currently running actor context.
The value returned is that which is returned by the
current behavior of @samp{NEAR-REFR} upon invocation (or, if
@samp{NEAR-REFR} chose to @samp{bcom} something else, the second
argument passed to its @samp{bcom}, or is @samp{unspecified?} if not provided.)

@node Asynchronous invocation
@subsection Asynchronous invocation

@example
(<- near-object arg ...)
;=> <local-promise>
@end example

Like @samp{$}, but called asynchronously and returns a @emph{promise},
which may be handled with @samp{on}.
Unlike @samp{$}, @samp{<-} is not limited to only @emph{near} objects;
interaction with @emph{far} actors are permitted as well.

@example
(<-np [actor-refr live-refr?] [arg any/c] ...)
@end example

Like @samp{<-} but does not return (or require the overhead of) a
promise.
@samp{<-np} is effectively an optimization; where promises are not
needed, using @samp{<-np} over @samp{<-} is a good idea.

@example
(on vow
    [fulfilled-handler]
    [#:catch broken-handler]
    [#:finally finally-handler]
    [#:promise? #f])
@end example

Sets up promise handlers for @samp{vow}, which is typically a
@samp{live-refr?} to a @emph{promise} but may be anything.

@samp{fulfilled-handler}, @samp{broken-handler}, and @samp{finally-handler}
all, if specified, may be either a procedure (the most common case)
which is run when @samp{vow} becomes resolved, or a reference to an
actor that should be messaged when the promise is resolved with the
same arguments that would be passed to an equivalent procedure.
As their names suggest, @samp{fulfilled-handler} will run if a promise is
fulfilled and takes one argument, the fulfilled value.
@samp{broken-handler} will run if a promise is broken and takes one
argument, the error value.
@samp{finally-handler} will run when a promise is resolved, no matter the
outcome and is called with no arguments.

If @samp{#:promise?} is set to @samp{#t}, then @samp{on}
will itself return a promise.
The promise will be resolved as follows:

@itemize
@item
If @samp{vow} is fulfilled and @samp{fulfilled-handler} is not
provided, the promise returned will share @samp{vow}'s resolution.
@item
If @samp{vow} is broken and @samp{broken-handler} is not
provided, the promise returned will share @samp{vow}'s broken result.
@item
If @samp{vow} is fulfilled and @samp{fulfilled-handler} is provided,
the resolution will be whatever is returned from @samp{fulfilled-handler},
unless @samp{fulfilled-handler} raises an error, in which case the promise
will be broken with its error-value set to this exception.
@item
If @samp{vow} is broken and @samp{broken-handler} is provided,
the resolution will be whatever is returned from @samp{broken-handler},
unless @samp{broken-handler} raises an error, in which case the promise
will be broken with its error-value set to this exception (which
may even be the original exception).
@end itemize

@node Vats
@section Vats

In order to start programming with Goblins, you will need to boot up a
vat somewhere.

In general, Goblins uses @uref{https://github.com/wingo/fibers/, Fibers} to implement vats.  However, it is
intended that users should be able to roll their own vats should they
have ``unusual'' event loop needs.  More on how to do this in a future
iteration of these docs.  ;)

@menu
* Starting and stopping vats::
* Rolling your own vats::
@end menu

@node Starting and stopping vats
@subsection Starting and stopping vats

@example
> (define my-vat (spawn-vat))
> my-vat
=> #<vat izvD0DJT>
@end example

Running @samp{spawn-vat} returns a vat object.  Code can be run within the
context of a vat using the @samp{call-with-vat} procedure.  This is useful
for bootstrapping vats, or hacking at the REPL:

@example
> (define my-vat (spawn-vat))
> (call-with-vat my-vat
   (lambda ()
     (define alice (spawn ^greeter "Alice"))
     ($ alice "Bob")))
=> "Hello Alice, my name is Bob!"
@end example

For convenience, the @samp{with-vat} macro can be used instead of
@samp{call-with-vat}.  Here's the above example rewritten to use
@samp{with-vat}:

@example
> (define my-vat (spawn-vat))
> (with-vat my-vat
   (define alice (spawn ^greeter "Alice"))
   ($ alice "Bob"))
=> "Hello Alice, my name is Bob!"
@end example

You can also use the @samp{vat-halt!} procedure to stop the vat, and
@samp{vat-running?} to check its status:

@example
> (vat-halt! my-vat)
> (vat-running? my-vat)
=> #f
@end example

@strong{NOTE:} In the future, we would like vats to automatically halt when
no more references exist and thus the vat can do no more work.  The
Racket version of vats works this way, it would be good for the Guile
version to do the same.  In the meanwhile, you'll need to call
@samp{vat-halt!} manually.

@node Rolling your own vats
@subsection Rolling your own vats

Goblins comes with a built-in vat implementation that is
general-purpose and useful in most situations.  However, using Goblins
with a foreign event loop (such as a GUI widget toolkit or game
engine) calls for a specialized vat that knows how to hook into it.
The API described below is considered unstable and likely to change in
future releases of Goblins, so be prepared to update custom vat code
at a later date.

The @samp{make-vat} constructor is used to make specialized vats.  Below is
some pseudocode that illustrates the basic pattern:

@example
> (define (make-custom-vat)
    (define thread #f)
    (define (start churn)
      (define (loop)
        (churn (dequeue-msg))
        (loop))
      (set! thread (call-with-new-thread loop)))
    (define (halt)
      (cancel-thread thread))
    (define (send msg return?)
      (if return?
          (enqueue-msg-and-wait-for-result msg)
          (enqueue-msg msg)))
    (make-vat #:start start
              #:halt halt
              #:send send))
@end example

How to implement @samp{enqueue-msg}, @samp{enqueue-msg-and-wait-for-result}, and
@samp{dequeue-msg} depends on the integration target and is left as an
exercise for the implementer.

Vat implementations must provide three hook procedures to @samp{make-vat}:

@itemize
@item
@samp{start}: This procedure starts the vat message processor in the
desired foreign event loop.  It accepts a @samp{churn} procedure as its
only argument.  The @samp{churn} procedure accepts a message object and
processes it using the vat's underlying actormap.
@item
@samp{halt}: This procedure stops vat message processing.  It is a thunk,
meaning that it accepts zero arguments.
@item
@samp{send}: This procedure receives a message to process within the vat.
It accepts two arguments: a message and a boolean flag indicating if
the result of processing the message needs to be returned to the
caller.  When the return flag is @samp{#f}, vat implementations can
simply queue the message for processing and move on.  When it is
@samp{#t}, however, the vat must arrange to wait and retrieve the
eventual result of processing the message.
@end itemize

Custom vat implementations @emph{must} enqueue and dequeue messages in a
thread-safe manner because incoming messages from other vats may be,
and likely are, running in a different thread.

The @samp{vat-start!} procedure initiates the processing of messages sent
to the vat.

@example
> (define custom-vat (make-custom-vat))
> (vat-start! custom-vat)
@end example

Once a custom vat has been started, it can be used as usual:

@example
> (define alice
    (with-vat custom-vat
     (spawn ^greeter "Alice")))
@end example

@node Actormaps
@section Actormaps

So far we have dealt with vats, but there is a lower level of
abstraction in Goblins which is called an @samp{actormap}.

The key differences are:

@itemize
@item
@strong{vat}: An event loop implementation that runs continuously
in its own thread.
Comes pre-built with tooling to handle message passing between
vats.
Actually wraps an actormap.
@item
@strong{actormap}: The transactional datastructure that vats use.
But, useful on its own to either build your own event loop
or as a synchronous one-turn-at-a-time mapping of actor references
to their current implementations.
@end itemize

The best way to learn is by doing, so let's make an actormap now:

@example
(define am (make-actormap))
@end example

Let's add an actor to it.
Since cells are simple, let's add one of those.

@example
(define lunchbox
  (actormap-spawn! am ^cell))
@end example

What's in the lunchbox?
We could take a look with @samp{actormap-peek}:

@example
> (actormap-peek am lunchbox)
; => #f
@end example

An empty lunchbox@dots{} we'd like to eat something later, so how about we
pack ourselves a nice sandwich by using @samp{actormap-poke}:

@example
> (actormap-poke! am lunchbox 'peanut-butter-and-jelly)
> (actormap-peek am lunchbox)
; => 'peanut-butter-and-jelly
@end example

What if we tried to look inside the lunchbox with @samp{actormap-poke!}
instead of @samp{actormap-peek}?

@example
> (actormap-poke! am lunchbox 'bbq-tofu)
> (actormap-poke! am lunchbox)
; => 'bbq-tofu
@end example

Well that worked just fine.
What happens if we do both operations with @samp{actormap-peek}?

@example
> (actormap-peek am lunchbox 'chickpea-salad)
> (actormap-peek am lunchbox)
; => 'bbq-tofu
@end example

What the@dots{}???
Our lunchbox didn't change!

We said that actormaps were transactional.
We have gotten a hint of this with the above:
@samp{actormap-poke!} returns a value and commits any changes.
@samp{actormap-peek} returns a value and throws any changes away.

Actually, these two functions are just simple conveniences that wrap a
more powerful (but cumbersome to use) tool, @samp{actormap-turn}:

@example
> (actormap-turn am lunchbox)
; => 'bbq-tofu
; => #<transactormap>
; => '()
(actormap-turn am lunchbox 'chickpea-salad)
; => *unspecified* (not printed)
; => #<transactormap>
; => '()
@end example

@samp{actormap-turn} returns three values to its continuation: a
return value (if any@dots{} Guile's REPL doesn't print out the unspecified
value, which is what actually got returned in the second invocation),
a ``transactormap'', and a list representing messages
queued for delivery.

This @samp{#<transactormap>} is interesting@dots{} we haven't mentioned this
before, but there are really two kinds of actormaps, ``whactormaps''
(weak-hash actormaps) and ``transactormaps'' (which hold a transaction
which we can choose whether or not to commit).
Actually our @samp{am} is a whactormap:

@example
> am
; => #<whactormap>
@end example

It's a weak hashtable which stores the current mutable state of all
actors.

But we'd like to see about that transactormap@dots{} just running
@samp{actormap-turn} on its own won't update the lunchbox either:

@example
> (actormap-turn am lunchbox 'chickpea-salad)
; => *unspecified* (not printed)
; => #<transactormap>
; => '()
> (actormap-turn am lunchbox)
; => 'bbq-tofu
; => #<transactormap>
; => '()
@end example

We need to capture the transactormap and commit it.

@example
> (define-values (_val tr-am _nm)
    (actormap-turn am lunchbox 'chickpea-salad))
> tr-am
; => #<transactormap>
> (transactormap-merge! tr-am)
@end example

Now at least our @samp{am} actormap should reflect that we've switched out
our @samp{'bbq-tofu} sandwich for a @samp{'chickpea-salad} one:

@example
> (actormap-peek am lunchbox)
; => 'chickpea-salad
@end example

Horray!

